---
title: Steamed Eggplant with Garlic (Syun Yeung Cing Keng Zi 蔥蒸茄子)
description: Soft steamed eggplant with a sizzling aromatic oil finish. Silky eggplant draped in a savory-sweet sauce that comes alive when the hot oil hits the garlic.
prep time: 10 minutes
cook time: 15 minutes
servings: 2
tags:
  - eggplant
  - vegetarian
  - steamed
  - chinese
source: https://thewoksoflife.com/steamed-eggplant
cuisine: Chinese
course: Main
---

> The sizzling oil technique is what makes this dish sing. The hot oil "cooks" the raw garlic and scallions without further steaming, releasing all that fragrant essential oils. It should bubble vigorously the moment it hits the aromatics.

== Steam the Eggplant ==

Cut the @long Asian eggplants{400%g} into 5 cm batons (or halve lengthwise if smaller). Arrange on a #heat-proof plate{}.

Place in a #steamer{} over boiling water and steam for ~{10%minutes} until completely tender—you should be able to pierce them easily with a chopstick.

== Dress the Eggplant ==

While the eggplant steams, mix the sauce in a small bowl:
- @light soy sauce{2%tbsp}
- @sugar{1%tsp}
- @toasted sesame oil{½%tsp}
- @warm water{1%tbsp}

Stir until the sugar dissolves. Carefully remove the plate from the steamer and pour the sauce over the eggplant, letting it absorb.

== Sizzling Oil Finish ==

Pile directly on top of the eggplant:
- @minced garlic{4%cloves}
- @chopped scallion{1%stalk}
- @diced red chili{1} (optional)

In a #small saucepan{}, heat @neutral oil{2%tbsp} until it is smoking hot. Carefully pour the hot oil directly over the garlic and scallion pile—you should hear an immediate sizzle. The heat will cook the aromatics just enough to release their fragrance without raw bite.

Serve immediately.